<h3>Three-line summary</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Kimi K3 is the <strong>largest open (open-weight) model ever</strong> — a brain size (parameter count) of 2.8 trillion.</li>
    <li>On several public tests (benchmarks) it sits <strong>third, just below Claude Fable 5 and GPT-5.6</strong>, and in some areas such as building web screens it is <strong>first</strong>.</li>
    <li>Yet its price is <strong>a tier cheaper</strong> than the top-ranked models. Because it broke the received wisdom that &ldquo;open models trail American paid models by six months to a year,&rdquo; the industry was thrown into a stir.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Five terms to learn first</h3>
<p>Know just these five and everything below reads.</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Plain explanation</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Parameter</strong></td><td>The <strong>number of adjustment screws</strong> inside a model. During training these screws turn little by little, and knowledge and ability get engraved into them. The more there are, the more the model can hold — at the cost of higher expense to build and run.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Token</strong></td><td>The <strong>smallest piece</strong> of text as an AI reads and writes. Roughly one word is 1&ndash;2 tokens. AI usage fees are also billed by token count.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Context window</strong></td><td>How much a model can <strong>hold in mind and work on at once</strong>. K3 has 1 million tokens — big enough to hand it several thick books whole and say &ldquo;read all of this and answer.&rdquo;</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Open weights</strong></td><td>Releasing the finished model&rsquo;s <strong>&lsquo;brain file&rsquo; (the weights) in its entirety</strong>. Anyone can download it, run it on their own server, and modify it. The opposite is closed — the OpenAI/Anthropic approach of hiding the file and selling only access.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Benchmark</strong></td><td>A <strong>report card from giving many models the same exam and grading it</strong>. There are many kinds — coding tests, general-knowledge tests, real-work tests — and every ranking in this article is a benchmark ranking.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h3>What happened</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Launch</strong>: 2026-07-16, Moonshot AI (Beijing). Usable right away through the app and the API (the paid access channel for developers).</li>
    <li><strong>Size</strong>: <strong>2.8 trillion</strong> parameters — the largest in the history of open models.</li>
    <li><strong>Memory</strong>: a <strong>1 million token</strong> context window, multimodality that also understands drawings and photos, and a three-level reasoning mode that dials thinking deeper or shallower.</li>
    <li><strong>Weights</strong>: at launch the model was usable only through the app and API; the brain file followed as promised and was <strong>published on Hugging Face on July 26–27</strong> (the evening of the 26th, US time). The license is not MIT but a custom <strong>Kimi K3 License</strong>: businesses that sell the model as a service (MaaS) at large scale need a separate agreement with Moonshot.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Secret ① — Calling only 16 of 896 experts (MoE)</h3>
<p>&ldquo;Wouldn&rsquo;t running 2.8 trillion parameters cost a fortune?&rdquo; — it would. That is why K3 uses a structure called <strong>MoE (Mixture of Experts)</strong>. Picture a large general hospital. There are 896 specialists, but not all 896 examine a single patient. The front desk looks at the symptoms and calls in <strong>only the 16 who are actually needed</strong>.</p>
<p>K3 works exactly like that. Every time one piece of text (a token) comes in, the <strong>router</strong> (the front desk) picks <strong>only 16</strong> of the 896 expert neural networks and puts them to work. Two always-on shared experts and common parts such as attention are added on top. So while the whole knowledge warehouse is a huge 2.8 trillion, the part actually working at any moment is <strong>about 104 billion (104B)</strong>, a little over 4% of the total — an elephant&rsquo;s knowledge with a cheetah&rsquo;s stride.</p>

