<h3>Secret ② — Reads long, and does not forget as it deepens (KDA · AttnRes)</h3>
<p>These are the other two improvements Moonshot described on its official blog. The names are hard, but what they do is simple.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Kimi Delta Attention (KDA)</strong> — while an AI reads text it keeps computing &ldquo;which word should I focus on&rdquo; (this is called <strong>attention</strong>), and the longer the text, the more this computation snowballs. KDA is a way of making that computation lighter, raising the speed of writing answers over long 1-million-token documents by <strong>up to 6.3×</strong>. It is like reading with a well-organized summary memo in hand instead of underlining the whole book.</li>
    <li><strong>Attention Residuals (AttnRes)</strong> — an AI model refines its thinking as it passes through dozens to hundreds of layers, and as the layers get deeper there is a problem: <strong>important information seen earlier gradually fades</strong>. AttnRes is a device that <strong>picks out and pulls back</strong> information from earlier layers when it is needed. It is similar to re-checking the original note partway through a game of telephone so the message does not get distorted.</li>
</ul>
<p>Thanks to these structural improvements, Moonshot explains, K3&rsquo;s <strong>&lsquo;fuel efficiency&rsquo; improved about 2.5×</strong> over its predecessor K2. That does not mean &ldquo;2.5× smarter&rdquo;; it means it <strong>extracts 2.5× more ability from the same amount of compute and training data</strong>.</p>

<p>In the official diagram Moonshot published, three things fit into one picture — <strong>Stable LatentMoE</strong>, which selects and uses experts, <strong>KDA</strong>, which reads long text lightly, and <strong>AttnRes</strong>, which pulls important information back across the blocks (the horizontal lines on the right).</p>

