<h4>First place chosen by people themselves: building web screens</h4>
<p><strong>LMArena</strong> is an unusual testing ground. It poses a question, <strong>shows two models&rsquo; answers side by side with the names hidden, and has people vote for the better one</strong>. Those wins and losses pile up as a score (Elo), like a chess ranking. In <strong>Frontend Code Arena</strong>, where models build web screens (frontends), K3 took <strong>first place with 1,679 points</strong> — beating Claude Fable 5. Since its predecessor K2.6 was 18th, it jumped <strong>17 places in a single generation</strong>, and it was first in 6 of the 7 subcategories (only in the games category was it second, behind Fable 5). When they faced off on the same question, the rate at which K3&rsquo;s answer was chosen (its win rate) averaged <strong>76%</strong> — meaning it won 76 out of 100 matchups.</p>
<p>It is strong at writing too. On one writing leaderboard that blind-grades real scripts (run by Louie), it took <strong>first place with 2,840 Elo</strong>, the first time an open model has taken the top spot on that board (its predecessor was 21st). On the <strong>Vals Index</strong>, which evaluates mainly practical work tasks, it was <strong>second (74.7%)</strong> out of 38 models.</p>

<h3>Hold on — you should not take self-measured scores at face value</h3>
<div class="note warn">
    <span class="nh">Same model, same test, a 7-point difference</span>
    The Terminal-Bench score of 88.3 in the table above is a number <strong>Moonshot measured itself</strong>. Yet when the evaluation firm Vals <strong>re-measured it independently, it came out around 80.9</strong>. Rather than trickery, this is because what tools the model was handed and what settings it was tested under — that is, the <strong>&lsquo;harness&rsquo; (the execution environment wrapped around the model)</strong> — differed. It is like the same driver posting a different lap time in a different car. The lesson: <strong>an AI report card must always be read together with &ldquo;who measured it, under what conditions.&rdquo;</strong>
</div>

<h3>Price — why everyone is talking about &ldquo;value for money&rdquo;</h3>
<p>An AI model&rsquo;s API is <strong>metered by the number of tokens</strong> you pay for. The question (input) and the answer (output) are each priced.</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Price (per million tokens)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Input</td><td>$3.00</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Input — cache hit</td><td>$0.30 <span class="scn-sub">(an automatic 90% discount when the same content is sent repeatedly)</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Output</td><td>$15.00</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>What this price means only becomes visible through comparison. As the industry sums it up, <strong>&ldquo;Opus 4.8-class performance at Sonnet 5 prices&rdquo;</strong> — that is, it sells a top-tier model&rsquo;s ability at the price of the tier below (Latent.space). On top of that, there is <strong>no surcharge</strong> even when you feed in a 1-million-token document. There is an opposite reading too: since it costs far more than its predecessor K2, some read it as a signal that &ldquo;<strong>the era of ultra-cheap Chinese AI is over</strong>&rdquo; (The Decoder). Both are two sides of the same fact — <strong>it is cheaper than the top tier and more expensive than the Chinese open models of the past.</strong></p>

<h3>What can you build with it — the official demos</h3>
<p>Moonshot&rsquo;s showcase focuses less on &ldquo;jobs that finish in one go&rdquo; and more on <strong>long-horizon autonomous work</strong> — the mode where a person hands over a task and walks away while the model plans, builds, checks results, and fixes on its own for hours or days.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Semiconductor chip design</strong> — running alone for 24&ndash;48 hours, it designed and even verified a small AI-dedicated chip with open design tools (4mm² of area, processing 8,700 tokens per second in simulation). In effect, an AI designed &ldquo;a chip to run AI on.&rdquo;</li>
    <li><strong>Scientific data analysis</strong> — it analyzed 391 gravitational-wave observation events (ripples in spacetime made by things like black hole collisions) by commanding more than 20 <strong>subagents</strong> (helper AI workers the main model directs) at once, and produced 7 kinds of visualizations plus summaries of more than 10 papers.</li>
    <li><strong>Games and 3D</strong> — a wuxia open-world RPG, a colosseum, a fighting game, a Game Boy emulator, a 3D typewriter that disassembles into parts, a black hole simulation and more, all built for the browser. Notably it uses a feedback loop — the same one human developers use — of <strong>taking screenshots of the screens it built, checking them with its own eyes, and fixing them</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Motion graphics and video</strong> — because one model handles text, images, and video together, it also produced things like an animated video explaining its own architecture.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Kernel optimization — speeding up the code that makes up its own body</h4>
<p>What developers paid the most attention to in the showcase was <strong>GPU kernel optimization</strong>. A kernel is a <strong>small, core computation program</strong> that runs on the GPU, and an AI model&rsquo;s speed is ultimately decided by how fast these kernels are. The experiment was designed like this — each model was put in <strong>the same sandbox</strong> and made to profile, rewrite, and benchmark <strong>4 kinds of kernel tasks</strong> (AttnRes, DSA, 512-head MLA, KDA) <strong>on its own for up to 24 hours</strong> (on an NVIDIA H200 and a third-party GPGPU). The result: <strong>K3 was first in 3 of the 4 tasks</strong>, with Claude Fable 5 first only on DSA. On the AttnRes task in particular, one forward + backward pass fell from <strong>283.6ms to 114.4ms</strong> — in effect, it optimized the code that makes up its own architecture (AttnRes, KDA).</p>

