<h3>How the world reacted</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>&ldquo;The era of catching up is over&rdquo;</strong> — OpenAI researcher roon commented, &ldquo;the era in which Chinese labs were far behind is over. Kimi is at least on par with today&rsquo;s leading open models.&rdquo; He added the caveat, though, that &ldquo;some people who actually use it will find it less useful than today&rsquo;s report card suggests.&rdquo;</li>
    <li><strong>Safety worries</strong> — with a closed model the company can cut off access if something goes wrong, but <strong>once open weights are out, they cannot be recalled</strong>. So &ldquo;should a model this strong be released at all, and in particular how good is it at hacking (cyberattacks)?&rdquo; instantly became a point of contention. Along with the observation that the cyber capability test (CyberGym) score is <strong>missing</strong> from the released report card, voices called for independent evaluation by bodies such as the UK AI Safety Institute (AISI), and there was also a counterargument that &ldquo;excessive fear is unnecessary&rdquo; (Transformer).</li>
    <li><strong>Even the stock market wobbled</strong> — reports followed that semiconductor and AI-related shares, which stood on the outlook that &ldquo;money will pour into expensive paid models and giant infrastructure,&rdquo; plunged right after the launch. It is being called a replay of the &lsquo;DeepSeek shock,&rsquo; when the cheap Chinese model DeepSeek shook the market in early 2025.</li>
</ul>

