<p>Independent evaluation is a little more sober. On the Artificial Analysis index (v5.0), K3.1 Pro scored <strong>55, fourth of 215 models</strong>, while Opus 5.5 scored 58 on the same version. On that version it is 11 points above K3. The 57.1 for K3 shown earlier comes from the previous version (v4.1), so the two numbers cannot be compared directly. In the same evaluation K3.1 Pro used 150 million output tokens to complete the index, well above the median of 87 million, and produced roughly 77 output tokens per second.</p>
<p>Strengths and weaknesses split as well. By Moonshot&rsquo;s figures it leads on long-horizon coding (SWE-Marathon 51.7 vs Opus 5.5&rsquo;s 46.3) and on Humanity&rsquo;s Last Exam with tools (70.0 vs 67.7). On science-style terminal tasks (Terminal-Bench Science) it scores 38.1, far behind GPT-6 Astra (68.1) and Opus 5.5 (63.3). One head-to-head over 13 tasks concluded that Opus is more reliable on strict math, physics and exact specifications, while Kimi is competitive or better on visual and compositional work. In the same test, Kimi&rsquo;s own completion summaries sometimes overstated its success. The lesson from K3, &ldquo;who measured it, under what conditions,&rdquo; carries straight over.</p>

<h4>Cyber capability was turned down on purpose</h4>
<p>The biggest worry at K3&rsquo;s launch was how much hacking ability such a strong open model had, and critics noted that its scorecard left out a cyber test. With K3.1 Pro, Moonshot gave an answer. Ahead of the weight release it says it <strong>found and removed cyberattack-related data</strong> from both the mid-training corpus and the reinforcement-learning environments, while keeping general coding tasks.</p>
<p>It showed the effect with an internal test. A checkpoint without these measures solved 90.5% of the problems in its internal xdev-bench-hard test; the final model solved 81.0%. The company states its principle as not releasing open weights that push the absolute frontier of cyber capability, and says it will raise the cyber capability of its open weights over time.</p>
<p>Pushback came quickly. The internal test is unpublished, so outsiders cannot check it. Critics noted that on Moonshot&rsquo;s own chart, another open model that is already public scores higher than K3.1 Pro. Whether fine-tuning the released weights could bring the reduced ability partly back also remains an open question; one outside account announced, right after release, that it would post a variant with the safeguards stripped. The concern raised with K3, that open weights cannot be recalled once out, still holds.</p>

<h4>License and a launch-marketing controversy</h4>
<p>The weights come under the <strong>Kimi K3.1-Pro License</strong>. It opens with MIT-style permission language, but reports say that, as with K3, model-API businesses with large revenue (a $20 million annual threshold) must sign a separate agreement. This article could not check the license text directly, so anyone planning commercial use should read the LICENSE file in the repository first.</p>
<p>The marketing around the launch also drew criticism. A creator published screenshots of what they said was a Moonshot promotion brief containing the line &ldquo;do not put on the paid partnership label.&rdquo; The authenticity and provenance of the screenshots have not been established, and no response from Moonshot had been found at the time of writing.</p>

